      "content": "The system is technically robust enough to prevent game-changing fraud, but it is not 100% verifiable for every single vote in a way that provides every voter with a personal \"checkmark\" of their individual mail-in ballot. Because the outcome relies on aggregated audits rather than counting every individual paper ballot back to a witnessable paper trail for each person, some legitimate security advantages over traditional on-the-day counting are inherently balanced against the public's ability to scrutinize every single transaction immediately. This creates a scenario where the overall result remains trustworthy, yet the lack of granular, real-time verification for each mailed vote can understandably fuel concerns about trust and oversight, even as current safeguards effectively stop large-scale manipulation.",
